<blockquote>
<p>Also why did you screw with the formatting here? It's not very significant here, but it also serves no purpose. Why not just use the normal format?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Authors generally develop signature methods of presenting articles that sometimes don't align with the usual <strong>Item #</strong>/<strong>Object Class</strong> layout. I know <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/not-a-seagull" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3009022); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3009022&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1586536198" alt="not_a_seagull" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3009022)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/not-a-seagull" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3009022); return false;">not_a_seagull</a></span> uses their own subclasses fairly regularly in their articles and I think <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1586536198" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;">MaliceAforethought</a></span> uses <strong>Item №</strong>/<strong>Anomaly Class</strong> sometimes.</p>
<p>It's a very slight deviation from the norm and it fits an author's headcanon. ¯\_(ツ)_/¯</p>

